CH_POEMS = [
("lore", "The Poems", "The Poems Behind This Book",
 ["This book leans on a handful of old texts, and you will meet their names on almost every page. Here is what they are. The Poetic Edda is a collection of about thirty old Icelandic poems about gods and heroes, such as Óðinn, Þórr, and Loki. Most of this book’s quotes and stories come from it. Nobody knows who wrote the poems.",
  "The Prose Edda was written by Snorri Sturluson, an Icelandic poet. He wrote it to preserve the style of the old poetry, retelling the myths in plain prose so that poets could keep using it. Many people believe his Christian beliefs influenced his writing, and that may be true to some extent. Still, scholars widely agree that he was genuinely preserving the old Norse stories, and they treat his work as a valuable resource for understanding the past.",
  "You will also see two other kinds of writing. Icelandic sagas are long prose stories about real families and kings. The rune poems are short verses that each describe one rune. The next pages list the poems you will meet most often, so you can look them up whenever a title puzzles you.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Poetic Edda and Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, the two collections described on this page."],
 "Which poem do you most want to understand? Write its name and why you chose it."),
("lore", "The Poems", "Poems of wisdom and the world",
 ["<b>Völuspá</b> (Voluspa), “the seeress’s prophecy.” A seeress tells Óðinn the story of the world, from its making to its ending at Ragnarök and its return.",
  "<b>Hávamál</b> (Havamal), “Sayings of the High One.” Óðinn’s advice on guests, friends, and keeping your wits. It also tells how he won the runes and lists the charms he knows.",
  "<b>Grímnismál</b> (Grimnismal), “Grímnir’s sayings.” Óðinn, in disguise and held captive, names the halls of the gods and the shape of the worlds.",
  "<b>Vafþrúðnismál</b> (Vafthrudnismal), “Vafþrúðnir’s sayings.” Óðinn and a wise giant test each other with questions about the world, its making, and its end.",
  "<b>Sigrdrífumál</b> (Sigrdrifumal), “Sigrdrífa’s sayings.” A valkyrie wakes and teaches the hero Sigurðr runes and counsel.",
  "<b>The rune poems.</b> Three medieval poems, Norwegian, Icelandic, and Anglo-Saxon, give each rune a short verse of its own.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> These poems are in the Poetic Edda and the medieval rune poems."],
 "Which of these poems offers advice you could use this week? Name it and one line of the advice."),
("lore", "The Poems", "Poems of the gods",
 ["<b>Lokasenna</b>, “Loki’s quarrel.” Loki crashes a feast and insults the gods.",
  "<b>Þrymskviða</b> (Thrymskvida), “the lay of Þrymr.” A giant steals Þórr’s hammer, and Þórr gets it back by dressing as a bride.",
  "<b>Skírnismál</b> (Skirnismal), “Skírnir’s sayings.” Freyr sends his servant Skírnir to win the giant maid Gerðr as his bride. Gods and giants often married in the old stories.",
  "<b>Hymiskviða</b> (Hymiskvida), “the lay of Hymir.” Þórr visits the giant Hymir for a great kettle and goes fishing for the world serpent.",
  "<b>Alvíssmál</b> (Alvissmal), “Alvíss’s sayings.” Þórr keeps a dwarf answering riddles until the sun comes up.",
  "<b>Hárbarðsljóð</b> (Harbardsljod), “Hárbarðr’s song.” Þórr trades insults with a ferryman, usually thought to be Óðinn in disguise.",
  "<b>Völundarkviða</b> (Volundarkvida), “the lay of Völundr.” A captive smith takes revenge on a king.",
  "<b>Hyndluljóð</b> (Hyndluljod), “the song of Hyndla.” Freyja questions a giantess about family lines.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> These poems are in the Poetic Edda."],
 "Which of these tales would you read aloud to someone you love? Name it and who you would read it to."),
("lore", "The Poems", "The Lokasenna: Loki’s quarrel",
 ["The Lokasenna is a flyting, a contest of insults, and it is the poem this book cites most when it speaks of Loki. The sea-giant Ægir (Aegir) brews ale for the gods and holds a feast. Þórr is away in the east. Loki has already killed one of Ægir’s servants and been chased out, but he walks back in and demands a seat.",
  "One by one he insults the gods, accusing them of cowardice and unfaithfulness. They answer him, and he has a cruel reply for each. At the end Þórr arrives and threatens him with his hammer, and Loki leaves. Two stanzas show the poem’s edge. First Loki reminds Óðinn of their bond: “Odin! dost thou remember when we in early days blended our blood together?” Then Þórr, on arriving: “Silence, thou impure being! My mighty hammer, Miollnir, shall stop thy prating. I will thy head from thy neck strike; then will thy life be ended.” (Stanzas 9 and 57, translated by Benjamin Thorpe, 1866.)",
  "A short prose ending in the manuscript tells what follows. The gods catch Loki, who had hidden as a salmon, and bind him with the entrails of his son. Skaði hangs a serpent over his face. His wife, Sigyn, holds a bowl to catch the venom. When she turns away to empty it, the venom drips on him, and his struggling shakes the earth.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Lokasenna and its prose ending are in the Poetic Edda. The excerpts are Benjamin Thorpe’s 1866 translation, now public domain."],
 "Think of a hard thing someone once said to you that held some truth. Write the words and the truth in them."),
]


CH_WELCOME = [
("lore", "Welcome", "How to say the names",
 ["Old Norse names look hard, but a few rules cover most of them. The letter Þ (þ) sounds like “th” in thin. The letter Ð (ð) sounds like “th” in this. An accent mark over a vowel means hold it a little longer. The letter ö sounds like the “u” in fur, and j sounds like English “y.” The final -r on names like Baldr and Heimdallr is a grammar ending, said roughly like “ur” (BAHL-dur, HAIM-dahl-ur). English spellings usually drop it, which is why you will also see Balder and Heimdall.",
  "<b>Óðinn</b> OH-thin<br><b>Þórr</b> THORR<br><b>Frigg</b> FRIG<br><b>Freyr</b> FRAYR<br><b>Freyja</b> FROY-ah<br><b>Týr</b> TEER<br><b>Loki</b> LOH-kee<br><b>Baldr</b> BAHL-dur<br><b>Heimdallr</b> HAIM-dahl-ur<br><b>Skaði</b> SKAH-thee<br><b>Jörmungandr</b> YOR-mun-gahn-dur<br><b>Fenrir</b> FEN-rir<br><b>Yggdrasill</b> IG-drah-sil<br><b>Ragnarök</b> RAHG-nah-rok<br><b>Æsir</b> AY-seer<br><b>Vanir</b> VAH-neer",
  "These are English-friendly approximations, not a perfect copy of how the Old Norse spoke.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Dr. Jackson Crawford’s YouTube channel, on Old Norse pronunciation, simplified for English readers."],
 ""),
]

